From 5a3d5e139befa3dc7ea3858cbef7124247c64749 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 11:02:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] player information: light levels of 0 are not given

MaxCLL and MaxFALL of 0 mean that they are not given, in HDR static
metadata (CTA-861.3) and in the level 6 of a Dolby Vision RPU. The
Dolby Vision page showed the L6 of such an RPU as "0 / 0 nits", which
reads as a film without light; the light levels of the film and of what
goes to the display said "not given" only where both were 0, and "1000 /
0 nits" where one was.

PLAYERINFO::LightLevelsText() says them all now: "1000 / 400 nits", the
word for a 0 ("1000 nits / not given", in the language of the interface:
Estuary #31834, the word that the page already says for them), and
"none" where both are 0, which the skin says as "not given" (#31834) as
it did for the others (HadesDVL6Var, HadesSrcCllVar, HadesOutCllVar). The
window system keeps the levels of the film as numbers and says them when
it publishes the page; the words come from Estuary's strings
(EstuaryWord(), which ColourWordsOf() now takes too).

On the video page the light levels of the film are named "MaxCLL /
MaxFALL", without "(file)", which the title of their column says ("The
film"), so that a level not given has room ("10000 nits / sin
indicar"). system/hades is made again by make-playerinfo.py.

Host test (outside the tree): test_info.cpp checks LightLevelsText()
(both given, both 0 in English and Spanish, one 0 of each side; it does
not build against the header before); 5 mutants are killed. t_none.py
(every place that shows the light levels, now also the L6, tests them
for "none" first, and the window system says the three with
LightLevelsText()): 7 faults before, none after. measure_all.py, with the
widest of a level not given: 0 that do not fit in en_gb, es_es, es_ar
and es_mx. WinSystemGbm.cpp, WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp and
DVBridgeGLES.cpp compile with the commands of the last build.
